    "explanationEn": "Release speed is the dominant factor in throwing distance. From the impulse-momentum relation, the gain in speed depends on force multiplied by the TIME over which it acts; from the work-energy relation, the gain in kinetic energy depends on force multiplied by the DISTANCE. A glide or rotation extends the path of force application from the arm alone to the whole body's travel, and lengthens the time correspondingly, so the same muscular force builds a higher release speed. One option is untenable, as technique cannot change the shot's mass; the third likewise, since after release the trajectory is fixed by speed, angle and gravity; and the fourth reverses the purpose, which is to accumulate speed rather than to save effort."
